```c
void _ox_err_set_with_location(Err         err,
                               const char *msg,
                               const char *xml,
                               const char *current,
                               const char *file,
                               int         line) {
    int xline = 1;
    int col   = 1;

    for (; xml < current && '\n' != *current; current--) {
        col++;
    }
    for (; xml < current; current--) {
        if ('\n' == *current) {
            xline++;
        }
    }
    ...
}
```

The issue: `current` (which is `pi->s`) can point to a position **before** `xml` (which is `pi->str`). This happens when `pi->s` is decremented below the start of the buffer. For example, in `read_text`:

```c
static void read_text(PInfo pi) {
    ...
    while (!done) {
        c = *pi->s++;
        ...
    }
}
```

And in `read_element`:
```c
} else { /* read as TEXT */
    char prev = *(start - 1);

    pi->s = start;
    if ('>' != prev && (' ' <= prev || is_white(prev))) {
        pi->s--;
    }
    read_text(pi);
```

Here `pi->s` can be decremented to point before `pi->str` if `start == pi->str`. Then when `read_text` encounters `\0` and calls `set_error(&pi->err, ..., pi->str, pi->s)`, the `current` pointer is before `xml`, and the loop `for (; xml < current && '\n' != *current; current--)` will read before the buffer.

